INT. POPPY'S KITCHEN - NIGHT

The whole street is dark. One candle on the counter, one torch in Poppy's mouth. She is on her knees, head inside an open cupboard.

POPPY
(muffled, torch in mouth)
I know I have matches. I bought matches. I remember the matches.

BEN
(he stands in the doorway holding a bag of frozen peas against his forehead)
You also remember buying a fire extinguisher and that was a dream.

POPPY
(she pulls out her head, spits the torch into her hand)
It was a very vivid dream.

BEN
(he steps forward, trips on a saucepan, catches himself on the fridge)
Okay. Okay. I'm fine. Who puts a saucepan on the floor?

POPPY
(pointing the torch at him)
Someone who was making you dinner before the lights went out.

BEN
(he looks at the pot, then at her, then at the pot)
That is soup?

POPPY
(she stands, wipes her hands on her jeans)
That is a risotto in its early years.

BEN
(he laughs, then stops laughing when he sees she is not laughing)
Poppy. I'm sorry. I love it. I love its early years.

POPPY
(she steps closer, the torch under her chin, spooky)
Say it again.

BEN
(he steps back into the table)
I love it.

POPPY
(softer, torch lowered)
No. The other thing. From the car.

BEN
(a long breath, peas slipping down his face)
I said I might be in love with you. Might. I said might.

POPPY
(she smiles, looks at the floor, then at him)
You said might very quickly.

BEN
(nodding, with great dignity)
I was driving.

CLOSE ON the candle. It flickers.

POPPY
(whisper)
Ben, I think I am also a bit of a might.

BEN
(he lowers the peas, finally)
Good. That's a good might. We can work with that.

The back door BANGS open. Cold air, flashlights swinging across the ceiling. THE TWINS, Poppy's nine-year-old nieces in matching raincoats, march in with a candle in each hand and a very large cake between them.

THE TWINS
Surprise!

POPPY
(she jumps and hits her head on the cupboard door)
Ow! It's not even my birthday!

THE TWINS
It is in the dark!

BEN
(to Poppy, quietly, through his hand)
Did you know about this?

POPPY
(rubbing her head, whispering back)
No, but I knew about the matches. They have matches.

THE TWINS
(together, holding up a box of matches)
We have matches!

BEN
(he takes a candle from one twin and lights it from the other)
Then I think this is a good night.

ANGLE ON the cake as the candles are lit one at a time. Poppy and Ben lean in together over the flame.

POPPY
(softly, only to him)
Make a wish.

BEN
(smiling, not looking away from her)
Already did.

BACK TO SCENE. The twins blow out the candles. The room goes black.

THE TWINS
(in the dark)
Again!
